+
+#### Pruning finished branches
+
+Finished branches and worktrees are pruned without anyone asking, so they do not pile up after every merged pull request.
+
+```bash
+ta cleanup --only branches --dry-run   # Every local branch, the verdict and the reason
+ta cleanup --only branches             # Delete the safe ones, ask about the rest
+```
+
+The deciding fact is the pull request state from your VCS adapter (GitHub through `gh`). `git branch -d` and `git cherry` are not used, because a squash merge rewrites the commits and makes a finished branch look unmerged.
+
+| Situation | What happens |
+|-----------|--------------|
+| PR merged and the branch has no commits beyond the PR head | Deleted without asking |
+| Branch tip is already contained in the default branch | Deleted without asking |
+| PR still open, no PR found, PR closed unmerged, or PR state unreadable | Question. Never deleted on its own |
+| PR merged but the branch has commits beyond the PR head (or the head is not available locally: run `git fetch`) | Question |
+| Branch is checked out in a worktree with uncommitted or untracked files | Question that lists the files. Nothing is removed |
+| Branch is checked out in a clean worktree | The worktree is removed first, then the branch |
+| The current branch, or the default branch | Never touched |
+
+Every deletion is recorded in `.ta/audit.jsonl` with the branch name and its tip SHA. To get a deleted branch back, run the command the entry prints: `git branch <name> <sha>`. The deletion only goes through if the branch still points at the commit that was checked, so a commit made while cleanup was running is never lost.
+
+Remote branches are never deleted by cleanup (turn on "Automatically delete head branches" in the repository settings instead). Remote branches whose PR was closed without merging are listed as findings, with the command to delete them yourself.
+
+This group runs on its own:
+
+- right after `ta draft watch` or `ta draft merge` sees the pull request merged, and
+- in the daemon's periodic cleanup, even when `auto = "off"` (with `auto = "safe"` it runs alongside every other group).
+
+Set `branches = false` under `[cleanup]` to switch both off. `ta cleanup --only branches` still works when you ask for it.
